WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:04.710
"Spider-Man: Across the Spider-Verse" has
received rave reviews, but some fans aren't

00:00:04.710 --> 00:00:07.560
happy about one particular aspect of the film.

00:00:07.560 --> 00:00:11.760
It goes without saying that "Spider-Man: Across
the Spider-Verse" is one of the most anticipated

00:00:11.760 --> 00:00:13.700
films of 2023.

00:00:13.700 --> 00:00:17.980
It's the follow-up to the Oscar-winning "Spider-Man:
Into the Spider-Verse" that's been five years

00:00:17.980 --> 00:00:18.856
in the making.

00:00:18.856 --> 00:00:23.859
It has quite a legacy to live up to, and if
reviews are any indication, it's soaring over

00:00:23.859 --> 00:00:25.802
most people's expectations.

00:00:25.802 --> 00:00:30.980
The animation is top-notch, and it takes the
characters in a darker, more mature direction

00:00:30.980 --> 00:00:34.491
with a plot that places the fate of the entire
multiverse at stake.

00:00:34.491 --> 00:00:38.629
However, it appears there's at least one thing
that's dampening people's enjoyment of the

00:00:38.629 --> 00:00:39.710
film in theaters.

00:00:39.710 --> 00:00:44.210
Numerous online threads have emerged of viewers
discussing how the sound mix felt off, at

00:00:44.210 --> 00:00:46.232
least at the beginning of the movie.

00:00:46.232 --> 00:00:51.210
In the official "Across the Spider-Verse"
discussion thread on Reddit, many people chimed

00:00:51.210 --> 00:00:55.940
in, implying this is a nationwide issue that
isn't localized to just a couple of theaters.

00:00:55.940 --> 00:00:57.690
For instance, one redditor wrote,

00:00:57.690 --> 00:01:02.521
"Did anyone else's theater have sound mixing
too low for characters compared to the music?

00:01:02.521 --> 00:01:05.145
Other than that, this movie was an absolute
treat."

00:01:05.145 --> 00:01:07.600
"My name is Miles Morales.

00:01:07.600 --> 00:01:09.400
I'm Spider-Man."

00:01:09.400 --> 00:01:13.920
Other people chimed in, saying they noticed
it during the opening sequence, which features

00:01:13.920 --> 00:01:17.209
a voiceover from Gwen Stacy while rhythmic
drumming plays.

00:01:17.209 --> 00:01:21.849
Apparently, the music's so loud it's difficult
to make out anything Gwen says, to the point

00:01:21.849 --> 00:01:24.869
where people are already looking forward to
the home release so that they can rewatch

00:01:24.869 --> 00:01:26.559
the scene with subtitles.

00:01:26.559 --> 00:01:30.490
Reddit isn't the only place where Spidey fans
are voicing their frustration.

00:01:30.490 --> 00:01:34.860
Similar comments have emerged on Twitter as
well as a thread on ResetEra, and it would

00:01:34.860 --> 00:01:38.473
appear the people behind the movie have become
well aware of the situation.

00:01:38.473 --> 00:01:42.930
"Across the Spider-Verse" writer and producer
Phil Lord tweeted some pictures of stickers

00:01:42.930 --> 00:01:46.140
he had made up to send to movie theater projectionists
that read:

00:01:46.140 --> 00:01:49.313
"I played Spider-Verse at full volume because
I'm awesome."

00:01:49.313 --> 00:01:50.642
He went on to explain,

00:01:50.642 --> 00:01:54.197
"The movie mix is really dynamic and comfortable
at full volume.

00:01:54.197 --> 00:01:57.460
In a full house it might even be better at
7.5!"

00:01:57.460 --> 00:02:01.860
It would appear not everyone has received
the memo yet, as plenty of people commented

00:02:01.860 --> 00:02:04.960
underneath Lord's post to talk about their
audio mix issues.

00:02:04.960 --> 00:02:08.890
The common complaint is that the dialogue
is too quiet when the film starts and gets

00:02:08.890 --> 00:02:13.530
drowned out by the music, and it seems to
be a recurring issue regardless of what format

00:02:13.530 --> 00:02:14.318
people see it in.

00:02:14.318 --> 00:02:14.818
"What?"

00:02:14.818 --> 00:02:15.649
"Oooo, okay."

00:02:15.878 --> 00:02:20.220
Some theater employees mentioned how they
would abide by Lord's guidelines, but it may

00:02:20.220 --> 00:02:22.340
take others some time to receive word.

00:02:22.340 --> 00:02:26.290
That means fans who saw it last night may
have had issues with the film that those who

00:02:26.290 --> 00:02:28.926
plan on seeing it in a week or two won't experience.

00:02:28.926 --> 00:02:32.659
Not being able to hear what Gwen's saying
at the beginning of "Across the Spider-Verse"

00:02:32.659 --> 00:02:36.802
can be cumbersome, but it's clear most fans
love this movie regardless.

00:02:36.802 --> 00:02:42.069
The film currently has a 95% positive rating
from critics on Rotten Tomatoes, and people

00:02:42.069 --> 00:02:44.139
can't stop raving about it on Twitter.

00:02:44.139 --> 00:02:49.020
Under Phil Lord's tweet, fans discussed their
personal experiences with the audio mix, but

00:02:49.020 --> 00:02:51.907
many were quick to remind him that the film
is fantastic.

00:02:51.907 --> 00:02:53.438
One person even tweeted,

00:02:53.438 --> 00:02:55.841
"It was too quiet in my theater tonight.

00:02:55.841 --> 00:02:57.581
Guess I'll have to go see it again.

00:02:57.581 --> 00:02:58.423
And again.

00:02:58.423 --> 00:02:59.277
And again."

00:02:59.277 --> 00:03:03.220
On top of that, the film is such a visual
spectacle that even scenes of just two characters

00:03:03.220 --> 00:03:05.610
talking are incredibly fun to watch.

00:03:05.610 --> 00:03:09.540
It makes sense there are people who pointed
out the audio issues but also argued that

00:03:09.540 --> 00:03:11.831
they came secondary to everything else.

00:03:11.831 --> 00:03:13.272
One person wrote on Reddit,

00:03:13.272 --> 00:03:16.973
"Eventually I stopped paying attention to
that because the movie sucked me in."

00:03:16.973 --> 00:03:21.230
Hopefully, projectionists get word of the
best settings to use for the film's audio.

00:03:21.230 --> 00:03:23.980
But if they don't, it seems like people will
still have a good time.

00:03:23.980 --> 00:03:27.590
"Spider-Man: Across the Spider-Verse" is playing
in theaters right now.

